    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "Valve threshold voltage, also called Uvalve. Forward voltage drop when the valve is conducting. Used in loss calculations, i.e. the switchLoss depend on numberOfValves * valveU0.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCConverter"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "valveU0",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#Voltage"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCConverterDCTerminal",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "A DC electrical connection point at the AC/DC converter. The AC/DC converter is electrically connected also to the AC side. The AC connection is inherited from the AC conducting equipment in the same way as any other AC equipment. The AC/DC converter DC terminal is separate from generic DC terminal to restrict the connection with the AC side to AC/DC converter and so that no other DC conducting equipment can be connected to the AC side.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "ACDCConverterDCTerminal",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#DCBaseTerminal"
      }
    ],
    "https://cimbology.com/ontology/cimdoc#cimPackage": [
      {
        "@value": "IEC61970.Base.DC"
      }
    ],
    "https://cimbology.com/ontology/cimdoc#packageTier": [
      {
        "@value": "Normative"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCConverterDCTerminal.DCConductingEquipment",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "A DC converter terminal belong to an DC converter.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCConverterDCTerminal"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "DCConductingEquipment",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCConverter"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCConverterDCTerminal.polarity",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "Represents the normal network polarity condition. Depending on the converter configuration the value shall be set as follows: - For a monopole with two converter terminals use DCPolarityKind \uFFFDpositive\uFFFD and \uFFFDnegative\uFFFD. - For a bi-pole or symmetric monopole with three converter terminals use DCPolarityKind \uFFFDpositive\uFFFD, \uFFFDmiddle\uFFFD and \uFFFDnegative\uFFFD.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCConverterDCTerminal"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "polarity",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#DCPolarityKind"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCTerminal",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "An electrical connection point (AC or DC) to a piece of conducting equipment. Terminals are connected at physical connection points called connectivity nodes.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "ACDCTerminal",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#IdentifiedObject"
      }
    ],
    "https://cimbology.com/ontology/cimdoc#cimPackage": [
      {
        "@value": "IEC61970.Base.Core"
      }
    ],
    "https://cimbology.com/ontology/cimdoc#packageTier": [
      {
        "@value": "Normative"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCTerminal.connected",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "The connected status is related to a bus-branch model and the topological node to terminal relation. True implies the terminal is connected to the related topological node and false implies it is not. In a bus-branch model, the connected status is used to tell if equipment is disconnected without having to change the connectivity described by the topological node to terminal relation. A valid case is that conducting equipment can be connected in one end and open in the other. In particular for an AC line segment, where the reactive line charging can be significant, this is a relevant case.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCTerminal"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "connected",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCTerminal.sequenceNumber",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "The orientation of the terminal connections for a multiple terminal conducting equipment. The sequence numbering starts with 1 and additional terminals should follow in increasing order. The first terminal is the \u0022starting point\u0022 for a two terminal branch.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACDCTerminal"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "sequenceNumber",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACLineSegment",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "A wire or combination of wires, with consistent electrical characteristics, building a single electrical system, used to carry alternating current between points in the power system. For symmetrical, transposed three phase lines, it is sufficient to use attributes of the line segment, which describe impedances and admittances for the entire length of the segment. Additionally impedances can be computed by using length and associated per length impedances. The BaseVoltage at the two ends of ACLineSegments in a Line shall have the same BaseVoltage.nominalVoltage. However, boundary lines may have slightly different BaseVoltage.nominalVoltages and variation is allowed. Larger voltage difference in general requires use of an equivalent branch.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "ACLineSegment",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#Conductor"
      }
    ],
    "https://cimbology.com/ontology/cimdoc#cimPackage": [
      {
        "@value": "IEC61970.Base.Wires"
      }
    ],
    "https://cimbology.com/ontology/cimdoc#packageTier": [
      {
        "@value": "Normative"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#ACLineSegment.Clamp",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
