  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDetailed",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "All synchronous machine detailed types use a subset of the same data parameters and input/output variables. The several variations differ in the following ways: - the number of equivalent windings that are included; - the way in which saturation is incorporated into the model; - whether or not \uFFFDsubtransient saliency\uFFFD (X\u0027\u0027q not = X\u0027\u0027d) is represented. It is not necessary for each simulation tool to have separate models for each of the model types. The same model can often be used for several types by alternative logic within the model. Also, differences in saturation representation might not result in significant model performance differences so model substitutions are often acceptable.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "SynchronousMachineDetailed",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDynamics"
      }
    ],
    "https://cimbology.com/ontology/cimdoc#cimPackage": [
      {
        "@value": "IEC61970.Dynamics.StandardModels.SynchronousMachineDynamics"
      }
    ],
    "https://cimbology.com/ontology/cimdoc#packageTier": [
      {
        "@value": "Normative"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDetailed.efdBaseRatio",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "Ratio (exciter voltage/generator voltage) of Efd bases of exciter and generator models (\u003E 0). Typical value = 1.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDetailed"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "efdBaseRatio",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#float"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDetailed.ifdBaseType",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "Excitation base system mode. It should be equal to the value of WLMDV given by the user. WLMDV is the PU ratio between the field voltage and the excitation current: Efd = WLMDV x Ifd. Typical value = ifag.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDetailed"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "ifdBaseType",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#IfdBaseKind"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDetailed.saturationFactor120QAxis",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "Quadrature-axis saturation factor at 120% of rated terminal voltage (S12q) (\u003E= SynchonousMachineDetailed.saturationFactorQAxis). Typical value = 0,12.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDetailed"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "saturationFactor120QAxis",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#float"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDetailed.saturationFactorQAxis",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "Quadrature-axis saturation factor at rated terminal voltage (S1q) (\u003E= 0). Typical value = 0,02.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDetailed"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "saturationFactorQAxis",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range": [
      {
        "@id": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#float"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDynamics",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
        "@value": "Synchronous machine whose behaviour is described by reference to a standard model expressed in one of the following forms: - simplified (or classical), where a group of generators or motors is not modelled in detail; - detailed, in equivalent circuit form; - detailed, in time constant reactance form; or - by definition of a user-defined model. It is a common practice to represent small generators by a negative load rather than by a dynamic generator model when performing dynamics simulations. In this case, a SynchronousMachine in the static model is not represented by anything in the dynamics model, instead it is treated as an ordinary load. Parameter details: - Synchronous machine parameters such as Xl, Xd, Xp etc. are actually used as inductances in the models, but are commonly referred to as reactances since, at nominal frequency, the PU values are the same. However, some references use the symbol L instead of X.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy": [
      {
        "@id": "https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61970.ttl"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label": [
      {
        "@value": "SynchronousMachineDynamics",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf": [
      {
        "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#RotatingMachineDynamics"
      }
    ],
    "https://cimbology.com/ontology/cimdoc#cimPackage": [
      {
        "@value": "IEC61970.Dynamics.StandardModels.SynchronousMachineDynamics"
      }
    ],
    "https://cimbology.com/ontology/cimdoc#packageTier": [
      {
        "@value": "Normative"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "http://iec.ch/TC57/CIM100#SynchronousMachineDynamics.SynchronousMachine",
    "@type": [
      "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment": [
      {
